Commentary of al-Nasafi (Madarik al-Tanzil) on surah 33, verse 10:

﴿When they came upon you﴾ is a substitute (badal) for "when they came upon you" (in the previous verse),

﴿from above you﴾, that is: from the upper side of the valley, from the direction of the east: the Banu Ghatafan;

﴿and from below you﴾, from the lower side of the valley, from the direction of the west: Quraysh;

﴿and when the eyes turned aside﴾, they inclined from their normal course and from the level of their gaze in bewilderment; or they turned away from everything, so that they looked at nothing except their enemy, because of the intensity of the terror;

﴿and the hearts reached the throats﴾. "Al-hanjarah" is the top of the adam's apple (ghalsamah), which is the end of the windpipe (hulqum). "Al-hulqum" is the passage for food and drink. They said: when the lungs swell from intense fear or anger, they rise, and the heart rises with their rising until it reaches the top of the throat. It has also been said that this is a metaphor for the agitation of hearts, even if the hearts did not literally reach the throats. It was narrated that the Muslims said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): "Is there something you can say, for the hearts have reached the throats?" He said: "Yes, say: O Allah, cover our faults and secure our fears."

﴿and you assumed about Allah assumptions﴾: this is an address to those who believed. Among them were those firm of heart and foot, and those weak of heart, who were on the edge (of faith), and the hypocrites. The first group thought that Allah was testing them, so they feared slipping and weakness in bearing it. As for the others, they assumed about Allah what has been related of them. Abu 'Amr and Hamzah read "al-zunun" without an alif, both in pause and in continuation, which is the analogical form. Madani, Shami and Abu Bakr read it with an alif in both cases, treating continuation as if it were pause. Makki, 'Ali and Hafs read it with an alif in pause only.

(p-21) Similar to this are "al-rasula" and "al-sabila", which they lengthened at the end of the verse-ending (fasilah), just as they lengthened it in the rhyme (qafiyah), as in the one who said:

"Cease your blame, O reproacher, and your rebuke..."

All of these are written with an alif in the Imam (the standard codex).
